1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,383
High in the Bolivian Andes, a particle passes through a tank of water.

2
00:00:05,383 --> 00:00:09,577
For an instant, the water gives off a faint flash of light.

3
00:00:09,577 --> 00:00:11,330
A sensor records it.

4
00:00:11,580 --> 00:00:17,000
The particle came from a shower set off by a cosmic ray striking Earth’s atmosphere.

5
00:00:17,250 --> 00:00:24,320
What could scientists learn by comparing measurements like this across an entire continent?

6
00:00:24,570 --> 00:00:29,054
The first connections grew inside individual countries.

7
00:00:29,054 --> 00:00:33,680
Chile’s university network was already operating in 1986.

8
00:00:33,930 --> 00:00:41,803
Brazil launched its national research networking project in 1989 and brought its first backbone online in 1992.

9
00:00:41,803 --> 00:00:46,170
Argentina’s universities were building links of their own.

10
00:00:46,420 --> 00:00:50,852
A researcher could now reach colleagues across the country.

11
00:00:50,852 --> 00:00:55,680
The next challenge was reaching the laboratory across the border.

12
00:00:55,930 --> 00:01:02,109
In 2003, Latin American research networks came together to form RedCLARA.

13
00:01:02,109 --> 00:01:09,890
A year later, its regional backbone began linking Argentina, Brazil, Chile, Panama and Mexico.

14
00:01:10,140 --> 00:01:18,340
The European Union helped fund this work through a project called ALICE, which also connected RedCLARA to Europe’s research network.

15
00:01:18,590 --> 00:01:27,460
A university could join through its national network and work with partners across Latin America and across the Atlantic.

16
00:01:27,710 --> 00:01:30,573
Now return to that tank in Bolivia.

17
00:01:30,573 --> 00:01:35,500
It belongs to the Latin American Giant Observatory, known as LAGO.

18
00:01:35,750 --> 00:01:40,372
Its detectors are spread across different latitudes and altitudes.

19
00:01:40,372 --> 00:01:42,370
Some sit near sea level.

20
00:01:42,370 --> 00:01:46,180
Others stand more than five thousand metres above it.

21
00:01:46,430 --> 00:01:52,859
At a mountain station, particles pass through less atmosphere before they reach the detector.

22
00:01:52,859 --> 00:02:00,370
Research networks let the teams bring their records together and compare what reaches the ground at each site.

23
00:02:00,620 --> 00:02:06,645
For years, research traffic between Brazil and Europe travelled via North America.

24
00:02:06,645 --> 00:02:13,340
In 2021, the European Union and its Latin American partners helped open a more direct route.

25
00:02:13,590 --> 00:02:20,550
Through the BELLA programme, research networks gained capacity on a submarine cable from Brazil to Portugal.

26
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:25,242
Physics teams in São Paulo and Lisbon put it to the test.

27
00:02:25,242 --> 00:02:33,170
They sustained a transfer of one hundred gigabits per second, ten times what the previous route had allowed.

28
00:02:33,420 --> 00:02:39,630
BELLA Two is extending that effort towards more research communities in Central and South America.

29
00:02:39,880 --> 00:02:42,379
Think back to the flash in Bolivia.

30
00:02:42,379 --> 00:02:46,330
A single detector can tell you what reached one mountaintop.

31
00:02:46,580 --> 00:02:54,440
Compare it with instruments at other heights and latitudes, and you can begin to see how cosmic radiation varies across a continent.

32
00:02:54,690 --> 00:02:59,150
That is a question no single station could answer.
